seeing headline after headline after headline and saying : ` I ca n't take it anymore -- I 'm getting out , ' \" <ei20 says> Kurt Brouwer of Brouwer amp Janachowski , a San Francisco investment adviser . The <ei21 withdrawals> could <ei22 spell> trouble for the $ 200 billion junk market . If the heavy <ei23 outflows> <ei24 continue> , fund managers will <ei25 face> increasing <ei26 pressure> to <ei27 sell> off some of their junk to <ei28 pay> departing investors in the weeks ahead . Such <ei30 selling> could <ei31 erode> prices of high-yield junk bonds , already <ei32 weakened> by a rash of corporate credit problems . Mutual fund groups have n't <ei33 lost> control of much of the outgoing money , <ei34 says> Louis Harvey , Dalbar 's president . Mutual fund officials <ei35 say> that investors have <ei36 transferred> most of it into their money market accounts , and to a lesser extent , government-bond funds . So the <ei38 impact> on the $ 950 billion mutual fund industry as a whole probably will be slight . But <ei39 tremors> are likely in the junk-bond market , which has <ei40 helped> to <ei41 finance> the takeover <ei42 boom> of recent years . Mutual funds are the among the largest holders of junk , accounting for more than a quarter of the entire high-yield , high-risk market . The 88 mutual funds investing solely in junk bonds hold assets of about $ 32 billion . Other funds hold a smattering of junk bonds , too . The $ 1.5 billion Fidelity High Income Fund has had a net <ei44 outflow> of about $ 150 million in the past two months . About $ 60 million <ei46 streamed> out last week alone , double the level of the week following last month 's Campeau Corp. credit <ei50 squeeze> . About 98 % of the outflow was <ei51 transferred> to other Fidelity funds , <ei52 says> Neal Litvack , a Fidelity vice president , marketing , with most <ei53 going> into money market funds . \" You get a news item , it hits , you have strong redemptions that day and for two days following -- then go back to normal , \" <ei54 says> Mr. Litvack . The fund , with a cash cushion of more than 10 % , has \" met all the redemptions without having to sell one thing , \" Mr. Litvack says . He <ei55 adds> : \" Our fund has had { positive } net <ei56 sales> every month for the last three years -- until this month . \" Vanguard 's $ 1 billion High Yield Bond Portfolio has <ei60 seen> $ 161 million <ei61 flow> out since early September ; $ 14 million of that <ei63 seeped> out Friday Oct. 13 alone . Still , two-thirds of the <ei65 outflow> has been <ei66 steered> into other Vanguard portfolios , <ei67 says> Brian Mattes , a vice president . The fund now <ei69 holds> a cash position of about 15 % . At the $ 932 million T. Rowe Price High Yield Fund , investors <ei70 yanked> out about $ 182 million in the past two months . Those <ei72 withdrawals> , most of which were <ei73 transferred> to other T. Rowe Price funds , followed little <ei74 change> in the fund 's sales picture this year through August . \" The last two months have been the whole ball <ei78 game> , \" <ei79 says> Steven Norwitz , a vice president . Junk-fund holders have barely <ei80 broken> even this year , as fat interest payments barely <ei83 managed> to offset declining prices . Through Oct . 19 , high-yield funds <ei85 had> an average 0.85 % total return ( the price change plus dividends on fund shares ) , <ei86 according> to Lipper Analytical Services Inc. That 's even less than the 4.35 % total return of the Merrill Lynch High - Yield Index . Fidelity 's junk fund has <ei87 fallen> 2.08 % this year through Oct . 19 , Lipper <ei90 says> ; the Vanguard fund <ei91 rose> 1.84 % ; and the T. Rowe Price fund <ei92 edged> up 0.66 % . People who <ei93 remain> in junk funds now could <ei95 get> <ei96 hit> again , some analysts and fund specialists <ei97 say> . Many funds in recent weeks and months have been <ei99 selling> their highest-quality junk issues , such as RJR Nabisco , to <ei100 raise> cash to <